/security-review — R7 round 1 — range 55be3bed..68c95758 (branch-scoped engine, filtered to the range)

NO FINDINGS — nothing in 55be3bed..68c95758 meets the bar.

- Checker path resolution (scripts/src/check.ts, rule 11 conditional:): entry.path is fed to globSync(entry.path, { cwd: config }) and only its match count is used; rule 15 builds one RegExp from a fixed key name and reads committed files under plugin.root. Input is this repo's own committed pack.yaml/config files, the tool is read-only, runs no subprocess, and writes nothing. A ../ glob would at worst count files outside config/ and emit a finding — no traversal that matters.
- Pre-commit exclude widening (.claude/, .venv/, lockfiles, etc.): the sec hook has pass_filenames: false + always_run: true and asks git for the staged set itself, so pre-commit's global exclude cannot blind it. Only detect-private-key (upstream hook) loses .claude/, and gitleaks still covers it on the same commit.
- gitleaks .turbo/*: a build-cache tree already gitignored in target repos; it holds hashed task outputs, not credential locations. paths allowlists apply only to that tree, matching the existing build/, dist/, .venv/ entries.
- fnox.local.toml gitignore removal: init's stack read carries the pinned provider slug and appends the provider row from the hygiene pack's conventions table (fnox.local.toml under an fnox banner) in every mode, including blank; a none answer appends nothing, which is correct since no such file exists. Residual risk is that the rendering is skill-driven prose, not code, and the fnox pack's file is designed to hold keychain references rather than plaintext — so even a missed section leaks nothing by default. Below the exploitability bar; worth a doctor predicate someday, not a finding here.
